<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:15:18Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Russia in Africa: What is Putin hoping to achieve?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2b0c23d08a967c52d6d58daba6cc4074ff7042349a3c287c03420547960ca07b</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:15:11Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:48:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/russia-in-africa-what-is-putin-hoping-to-achieve/a-79781858--24074440aebf.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/russia-in-africa-what-is-putin-hoping-to-achieve/a-79781858?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Following its invasion of Ukraine and the resulting rift with the West, Russia has declared that it intends to make a "historic return" to Africa. What have we seen of this so far?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Africa's first Youth Olympics open in Senegal amid hope and scrutiny</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:97d9c27bc25dd2b31c66d2159d24b5f67c76be07c9495d97a048bb6299f96ab3</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:15:18Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T09:08:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/africa-s-first-youth-olympics-open-in-senegal-amid-hope-and-scrutiny/a-79760478--c3155dbcff43.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/africa-s-first-youth-olympics-open-in-senegal-amid-hope-and-scrutiny/a-79760478?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Senegal is hosting Africa's first Youth Olympics, with 2,700 young athletes competing in Dakar, Diamniadio and Saly. The Games bring pride and hope, but also questions over cost, rights and legacy.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Egypt orders closure of independent news site Mada Masr</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5c0f67fa80e62ded9615cdcb19ab9bf8bed0fe137309aec806784e5daacb413b</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:14:01Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T23:16:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/egypt-orders-closure-of-independent-news-site-mada-masr/a-79781842--499fd97538eb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/egypt-orders-closure-of-independent-news-site-mada-masr/a-79781842?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Editor-in-chief Lina Attalah was briefly detained and fined over licensing violations. Rights groups condemned the ruling as a major blow to press freedom in Egypt, where independent media face growing pressure.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>DR Congo reports over 10,000 Ebola cases as the outbreak spreads abroad, with no approved vaccine or specific treatment for the Bundibugyo virus</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5056c70dc7a347f5996e39c25ae05c6fad11861020ce8aea31a1e4e995686af1</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:14:01Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T11:15:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/dr-congo-reports-over-10-000-ebola-cases-as-the-outbreak-spreads-abroad-with-no-approved-vaccine-or-/a-79773667--055ba7810ea4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/dr-congo-reports-over-10-000-ebola-cases-as-the-outbreak-spreads-abroad-with-no-approved-vaccine-or-specific-treatment-for-the-bundibugyo-virus/a-79773667?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">DR Congo has reported more than 10,000 Ebola cases and nearly 4,900 deaths. The outbreak involves a virus for which there is no approved vaccine or specific treatment, complicating efforts to contain its spread.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>'Tinubu Must Go!': Nigeria's northern protests test limits of political dissent</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:67de2340351693439c60ac10c604d7a9bb7bfdd5dba0c0bd981fbaff399bf7f6</id><updated>2026-10-28T15:14:01Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T14:20:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/tinubu-must-go-nigeria-s-northern-protests-test-limits-of-political-dissent/a-79713785--b9a2ff0fa9c4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/tinubu-must-go-nigeria-s-northern-protests-test-limits-of-political-dissent/a-79713785?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The arrest of several men wearing anti-government T-shirts and the disruption of a political meeting have renewed debate over free expression and protest rights in Nigeria.</summary><category term="World" /></entry></feed>
